---
title: "Algorithm debug mode"
description: "How a `::: {.debug}` block turns a real run into a steppable trace: why reads are computed rather than traced, the frame-ordering rule, and the two capture adapters that feed one shared frame shape."
---

A `::: {.debug}` block ([user guide](../guide/using/debug.tmd)) looks like one feature
from the reading side, forward/back/scrub through a recorded run, but it is built from
two genuinely different capture paths that agree on one wire format. This chapter is
about the parts of that agreement, and the two capture paths, that are not obvious from
reading the widget alone.

## One frame shape, two producers

Both capture paths agree to hand the client a `{frames, truncated, cap}` value: `frames`
is an array of

```json
{ "line": 4, "event": "line", "depth": 1, "func": "bubble",
  "locals": { "a": [5, 2, 8], "i": 0, "j": 0 },
  "changed": { "a": { "writes": [0, 1], "reads": [] } },
  "stack": [{ "func": "bubble", "line": 4 }],
  "stdout": "" }
```

per recorded step. `debug.js`'s `mount`/`renderStage`/`renderVars`/`diffLocals`-reading
code never asks which adapter produced a frame; it only ever reads this shape.

```{mermaid}
flowchart LR
  py["#| trace: true\n(Python cell)"] -->|sys.settrace, at build time| harness["trace_py.rs harness"]
  harness -->|Output::Rich JSON blob| shared["one {frames, truncated, cap}\nshape"]
  js["//\| trace: true\n({js} generator)"] -->|drain(), in the reader's browser| adapter["debug.js's JS adapter"]
  adapter --> shared
  shared --> mount["debug.js mount()\ntransport + cursor + vars + stage"]
```

**The Python path runs once, at build time**, under `sys.settrace`, and ships its
recording as static JSON. **The JS path runs in the reader's browser**, once per capture,
and can re-run: a traced `{js}` cell's own `//| input:` names make it re-capture (see
[the hidden-input bridge](#the-hidden-input-bridge-shared-with-scrolly)), which is the
whole reason the JS adapter exists over the Python one, the reader can change an input
and watch a fresh trace.

## Why the trace rides as an `Output::Rich` blob

`trace_py.rs`'s harness does not add a new output kind. It runs the author's code under
`sys.settrace`, builds the frame list, and calls IPython's own
`display(HTML('<script type="application/json" class="tali-debug-trace">…</script>'))`,
the same `display_data` mechanism a matplotlib figure or a `pandas` `DataFrame` uses. From
[the execution chapter](execution.tmd#from-a-kernel-message-to-html)'s own mapping, a
`display_data` message becomes `Output::Rich(html)`, an ordinary rendered-HTML output the
executor already knows how to splice after a cell and the freeze cache already knows how
to key and store.

That is the entire reason `freeze.rs` needed zero changes for this feature: a trace is,
from the cache's point of view, indistinguishable from a `<img class="tali-fig">` or a
`<pre class="tali-stream">`. The [cumulative-hash cache](execution.tmd#the-persistent-cache-freeze)
stores it, restores it on an unchanged rebuild, and busts it exactly when the traced
cell's own code (or anything upstream of it) changes, all for free. Toggling `#| trace:
true` on an already-cached cell changes that cell's code as the cache sees it (the
directive line is part of what gets hashed), so it busts the key and re-runs rather than
replaying a plain, untraced output under a traced request.

## Why `reads` are statically derived, not traced

`sys.settrace` observes **bindings**: a `line`/`call`/`return`/`exception` event, and the
frame's locals at that moment. It has no event for "this expression was evaluated",
so `a[j] > a[j + 1]` produces no signal at all, nothing is assigned, from the tracer's
perspective the line might as well not have subscripted anything. `writes` can still be
computed indirectly (diff this frame's locals against the previous frame's), but `reads`
structurally cannot: there is nothing to diff against, because reading never mutates
anything trace can see.

The harness works around this **statically**, once, before the code ever runs:

- Parse the cell's source with `ast.parse`.
- Walk every `Subscript` node whose target is a plain `Name` (`a[j]`, not
  `self.a[j]` or `f()[j]`) and whose context is `Load`, an assignment target
  (`a[j] = …`) parses to the identical node shape with `ctx=Store`, and excluding it is
  what stops a pure write from reporting itself as a read of the slot it is about to
  overwrite.
- Keep only a **whitelisted index expression**: a `Name`, a literal, `+`/`-`/`*` over
  those, or a unary minus. Anything else (a function call, an attribute access) is
  dropped, a read that cannot be evaluated safely and deterministically is not guessed
  at, it is simply not reported.

At trace time, `reads_for(line, locals)` re-evaluates each whitelisted index expression
for the current line against that frame's own live locals, sandboxed (`eval` with an
emptied `__builtins__`, so it can only ever do arithmetic over names already bound in
this frame) and wraps a caught exception (an index expression that referenced a name not
yet bound on this particular line) as simply "no read to report" rather than a crash.

One case earns special handling: `a[0] += 5` also parses its target as a `Subscript`
with `ctx=Store`, but unlike a plain assignment it genuinely reads the slot first
(read-modify-write). The harness collects every `AugAssign` target by node identity up
front so this one `Store`-context `Subscript` is treated as a real read, without
loosening the exclusion for every other assignment target.

## The frame-ordering rule

`sys.settrace`'s `line` event fires **before** that line executes. The harness records
one frame per such event, so a frame's own `line` is always the line about to run, never
the line that just ran, and a frame's `changed` (diffed against the *previous* frame's
locals) describes what running the *previous* line did. This is easy to get backwards
when reading the JSON cold, so it is worth making concrete. Traced verbatim:

```python
x = 1
x = 2
```

produces

| frame | `line` | `locals` | `changed` |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 0 | 1 (`x = 1`, about to run) | `{}` | `{}` (nothing has run yet) |
| 1 | 2 (`x = 2`, about to run) | `{x: 1}` | `{x: {from: null, to: 1}}` (line 1 just ran) |

Frame 0's `locals` is empty precisely because `x = 1` has not executed yet when frame 0 is
recorded, and frame 1's `changed` describes line 1's effect, not line 2's, because line 2
has likewise not executed yet. The client's line cursor (`focusLine`, reusing the
`.tali-hl-ln-hl` contract highlighting already ships for static code) highlights
`frame.line` unmodified; the variables panel's changed-value tint reads `frame.changed`
unmodified. Neither remaps anything, so this ordering is load-bearing all the way to the
DOM: get it backwards in a future adapter and the cursor will sit one line ahead of what
the reader believes just executed.

## The hidden-input bridge shared with `.scrolly`

A `.debug` block with `name="…"` gets one extra element server-side, a hidden
`<input type="hidden" class="tali-debug-input" data-tali-input="…">`. That is not a
bespoke reactive primitive invented for this feature: it is the exact same bridge
`.scrolly` already uses for `data-scrolly-state` (`scrolly.js`'s own comment names it
directly), a `data-tali-input`-tagged element whose `value` `registerInput`
(`tali-js.js`) already watches, publishing it as a reactive input the moment its `input`
event fires. Stepping the debugger, then, is just another way a `data-tali-input` element
changes value; a `{js}` cell downstream reacts with an ordinary `//| input: name`, no new
graph edge type, no code in `tali-js.js` aware that `.debug` exists at all.

`debug.js`'s `apply(i)` writes the bridge **last**, after the line cursor, the variables
panel and the stage have all been re-rendered, so a view cell that re-runs synchronously
off that `input` event reads an already-settled `window.taliDebug` registry rather than
one mid-update.

That publish is gated on a value change (`bridge.value !== String(idx)`), which is right
step-to-step, a re-render that lands on the same index should not spam a downstream cell,
but it has one sharp edge worth knowing if you touch this code: the very **first**
publish, always index 0, compares against the bridge's own server-rendered initial value,
which is also the string `"0"`. Left alone, that comparison is equal, so the first publish
is silently skipped, and a view cell that already ran once (as part of the page's initial,
document-order reactive pass, which runs before *any* enhancer's own mount, `.debug`'s
included) never hears that real data landed at all, until the reader manually steps.
`mount`/`recapture` track this with an explicit `forcePublish` flag rather than folding it
into the value comparison, so the ordinary step-to-step skip (a `Play` tick that lands on
an unchanged index) stays intact.

## The yield scanner's refuse-rather-than-guess contract

The JS adapter's traced source needs a line stamped onto every `yield`, so the client
knows which source line a captured step belongs to, the *displayed* code is never
rewritten (a reader must never see a synthetic call in code they wrote), so the stamp
rides in a separate `data-tali-js-src` attribute instead: `stamp_yields`
(`crates/core/src/render/yield_scan.rs`) rewrites `yield EXPR` to
`yield __at(LINE, EXPR)` in that hidden copy only, and `tali-js.js`'s `__at` records the
line and passes the value through unchanged.

Getting this wrong output is worse than getting it absent: `__at`-wrapping a `yield`
that only *looks* like a real one (one built inside a string, a comment, a regex
literal, or an unterminated string that swallows the rest of the file) would either
inject a genuine syntax error into an author's cell or silently invent a step the source
never intended. `stamp_yields` is a byte-level lexer state machine (`Code`, `Single`,
`Double`, `Template`, `LineComment`, `BlockComment`, `Regex`) precisely so it can tell a
real, top-level `yield` apart from a `yield` mentioned inside any of those. When it hits
a case it cannot resolve confidently, an unterminated string or block comment, `Regex`
inside an unterminated string, and so on, it returns `None` rather than a best guess.

The caller's fallback (`emit.rs`) is deliberately visible, not silent: `stamp_yields(&literal).unwrap_or_else(|| literal.clone())`
ships the **original, unstamped** source when scanning refuses. The cell still runs, it
is still valid JavaScript, the widget still mounts and steps, only the line cursor stays
parked rather than tracking a `yield` the scanner could not locate confidently. A
mis-scoped scanner can produce a *worse cursor*, never a *corrupted program*: the one
thing this contract forbids is inventing a `yield` that was not there. The scanner
extends this same discipline one level down, inside a yielded expression itself
`expr_end` tracks string/template literals and bracket depth but deliberately not
comments or regex, a documented simplification given the project's realistic corpus of
short snapshot yields (`yield { a, i, j }`), rather than a scan sophisticated enough to
reintroduce the same "confidently wrong" risk the whole state machine exists to avoid.

